\title{A Note on Sigma Protocols and Schnorr}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-21}

\p{
    [Sigma protocols](dhsorens-004U) are the three-move public-coin proof of knowledge, and
    [Schnorr](dhsorens-004V) is that shape worked through in full — the verification equation, the
    one subtraction that extracts the witness, and the simulator that is the same equation read
    backwards. Writing it out settled a naming question I had been sloppy about.
    \em{Zero-knowledge proof of knowledge} under-specifies Schnorr twice: the zero-knowledge is only
    \em{special honest-verifier}, and \em{of knowledge} is the half doing the work, because every
    group element has a discrete logarithm and so plain soundness is a claim about nothing.
}

\p{
    The pages:
    \ul{
        \li{[Sigma protocols](dhsorens-004U) — the relation, the three messages, and completeness,
        special soundness, and special honest-verifier zero knowledge as the three defining
        properties. A sigma protocol is the #{\mu = 1}, #{k_1 = 2} case of the
        [transcript trees](dhsorens-004K) the rest of the corpus runs on.}
        \li{[The Schnorr protocol](dhsorens-004V) — the arithmetic, why each of the three properties
        holds, and why prime order is what makes extraction work.}
        \li{[Cyclic groups and the discrete logarithm](dhsorens-004W) — a stub, opened first in the
        [algebraic toolkit](dhsorens-002J), for the group setting these protocols are stated over.}
    }
    What is still open is the gap between honest-verifier and malicious-verifier zero knowledge,
    which the three moves do not close on their own, and which none of these pages does more than
    name.
}
